{% import "_macros.j2" as m %}
{# The labeled set — one template for what used to be three (`concept-breakdown`, `card-row`,
   `icon-list`). They were never three *shapes*: all three are "N parallel labeled concepts", and
   the catalog already said to pick between them by count and body length. Three Match rules for
   one shape is what made this the family the fill misclassified most, and a wrong pick there costs
   real things (a set demoted to `card-row` loses nothing visually, but one demoted the other way
   loses its per-concept icon or its prose room).
   Now the *shape* is the classification and the arrangement is a field: `format` picks between the
   three presentations — `grid` (cards), `editorial` (flat) and `row`. All three are **grids**: a
   labeled set is N *parallel* concepts, and parallel concepts read side by side. The old fourth
   format, `list`, stacked them one under the other in a single column, which spent the whole slide
   width on one item and made a set of peers read as a sequence — it is gone. A legacy model that
   still carries `format: "list"` (or the `icon-list` id) falls through to `grid` below rather than
   failing; a set whose per-item prose genuinely needs a full-width column is not this shape at all
   (→ `content-text`, or split the slide). #}
{% set fmt = s.format or ('row' if s.template == 'card-row' else 'grid') %}
{% set items = s.cards or s.rows or [] %}
{% call m.stage(s) %}
{{ m.lead(s) -}}
{% if fmt == 'editorial' %}
{# The same labeled set with the card chrome removed — a flat composition on the canvas, for the
   sets where the panel carried no meaning and only cost room. Markup is deliberately the plain
   icon + label + body triple: everything that makes it a *composition* (how many columns, where a
   partial last row sits, how the type steps down when it gets tight) is count-driven CSS. #}
<div class="editgrid">
{% for c in items %}
<div class="eitem{{ m.frag(s) }}">{{ icon(c.icon) }}<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3>{% if c.body %}<p>{{ c.body }}</p>{% endif %}</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% elif fmt == 'row' %}
<div class="cardrow">
{% for c in items %}
<div class="rcard{{ m.frag(s) }}">{{ chip(c.icon) }}<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3><p>{{ c.body }}</p></div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% else %}
{# `grid` is also where a legacy `list` model lands, so it renders what that format rendered: an
   item whose `body` is empty (a bare anaphora) becomes a label-only card rather than a card with
   an empty paragraph in it. The lead is emitted above, for every format. #}
<div class="cards">
{% for c in items %}
<div class="ccard{{ m.frag(s) }}">{{ icon(c.icon) }}<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3>{% if c.body %}<p>{{ c.body }}</p>{% endif %}</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% endif %}
{% endcall %}
